From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patryk Kowalczyk <papyszoo@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:15:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: a long frame says what the emulated cores were doing,
 and the AX feed gap shows a drained queue

The 16:52 session's warp-in windows hold one 80.2 ms and one 70.4 ms frame, and the audio in flight
at the device is 58-64 ms. Cemu queues one 3 ms AX frame per turn of core 1's idle loop only, so a
frame on that core longer than the queue drains it. Pumping from another idle core does not help:
the AX thread is affined to the core that called AXInit (core 1 for this title), and a core other
than the one running the AX thread cannot make it runnable sooner, so nothing is queued while that
core sits in a host call. The pump is therefore Cemu's own again, unchanged.

What the build adds instead is the measurement. One [stall] line per frame over 34 ms (at most one
every 200 ms) with this frame's own numbers: the GPU thread's time waiting for the game, the stereo
pass's record and replay, the six GPU waits, and the AX feed gap. The record and replay counters are
zeroed every 5 s by the [draws] line on the same thread, later in the frame, so a swap just after
that zeroing no longer subtracts across it and prints a stereo pass of 1.8e13 ms. A second line
samples, at that instant, what each emulated core is doing: running the guest's code (its thread's
name and priority), inside a host call (the HLE export's name, how long it has been inside), idle,
or blocked on a GPU timestamp. It also says whether the AX thread's frame is queued and not taken,
which is the AX thread runnable and not scheduled.

The audio output thread, which already runs every grain (~5.3 ms), prints one [audio] line when no
AX frame has been fed for 40 ms, with the same per-core sample: the drain at a warp is then visible
even when no frame on the GPU thread crossed 34 ms. The [perf] line keeps the AX feed gap for its
10 s window (frames, longest gap between frames and blocks, skips), and the AudioOut queue line
keeps the lowest fill and the longest grain wait.

Cost on the guest's path: a handful of relaxed atomic stores per HLE call and per thread switch;
the sampling is reads only, from threads that already exist. Built, untested on console.
